meta {
  name: 06 Simulate provider webhook
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/webhooks/payments
  body: text
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:text {
  replaced by the pre-request script
}

script:pre-request {
  // The signature must cover the EXACT bytes sent, so the body is built here
  // as a string and signed as that same string. Letting Bruno serialise a JSON
  // body and signing something else would fail verification, which is the
  // property this request exists to show.
  const crypto = require('crypto');

  const secret = bru.getEnvVar('fakeWebhookSecret');
  const outcome = bru.getEnvVar('webhookOutcome') || 'SUCCEEDED';
  const raw = JSON.stringify({
    reference: 'fake_' + bru.getEnvVar('paymentId'),
    outcome: outcome,
  });

  req.setBody(raw);
  req.setHeader(
    'x-fake-signature',
    crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret).update(raw).digest('hex'),
  );
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.received: eq true
}

docs {
  Plays the payment provider: sends the event Notch Pay will send once real
  money has moved. Run `02 Create payment` and `05 Start checkout` first.

  The server must run with PAYMENT_PROVIDER=fake and the SAME secret as
  `fakeWebhookSecret` in the Bruno environment (32+ characters).

  ## What happens

  The API verifies the `x-fake-signature` header over the raw body, finds the
  payment by its reference (`fake_<paymentId>` with the fake), and hands it to
  PaymentActivationService. That one function grants access; the webhook
  writes nothing itself.

  Expected response: `{ "received": true, "outcome": "ACTIVATED" }`.

  Then check `Center Subscription / 01 Get subscription`: plan PAID,
  `paidUntil` about 30 days out, and `Center Subscription / 02 Get usage`
  shows the seats bought, at the price the payment line carried.

  ## The checks that matter

  1. SEND IT AGAIN, unchanged -> 200 with `outcome: "ALREADY_ACTIVE"`.
     `paidUntil` must not move. A provider that redelivers must not buy the
     center a second month.

  2. WRONG SECRET -> 401 WEBHOOK_SIGNATURE_INVALID. Change
     `fakeWebhookSecret` in the environment only (not on the server) and send.
     Nothing changes in the database.

  3. TAMPERED BODY -> 401. Comment out the `req.setHeader` line, set the
     header by hand from a FAILED body, and send a SUCCEEDED body. The
     signature covers the bytes, so the edit is caught.

  4. A FAILURE AFTER THE SUCCESS -> 200 with `outcome: "UNCHANGED"`. Set the
     environment variable `webhookOutcome` to FAILED and send. The payment
     stays SUCCEEDED and access stays on: a late, out-of-order failure must
     never undo money that has moved.

  5. A FAILURE BEFORE ANY SUCCESS -> `outcome: "MARKED_FAILED"`. Use a fresh
     payment. A later success still activates it — the provider is the
     authority on whether money moved.

  6. AN UNKNOWN REFERENCE -> 404 PAYMENT_REFERENCE_UNKNOWN, so the provider
     retries. Send before running `05 Start checkout` to see it.

  7. NO PROVIDER CONFIGURED -> 503 PAYMENT_PROVIDER_NOT_CONFIGURED. Restart the
     server without PAYMENT_PROVIDER and send.

  ## If Bruno refuses `require('crypto')`

  Compute the signature outside Bruno and paste it into the header:

      node -e "const c=require('crypto');const raw=JSON.stringify({reference:'fake_<paymentId>',outcome:'SUCCEEDED'});console.log(raw);console.log(c.createHmac('sha256','<secret>').update(raw).digest('hex'))"

  Send the printed body byte for byte, with the printed signature.

  Reset `webhookOutcome` to empty (SUCCEEDED) when you are done.
}
